            <label class="check-label">
              <input type="checkbox" id="p5-mvp-contain" name="p5-mvp-contain" data-check-id="mvp-contain" />
              <span class="check-num">21</span>
              <span class="check-title">The final decision rests on containment evidence</span>
            </label>
            <div class="check-detail">Evidence looks like: the log's verdict resting on artifacts — before/after manifest receipts, target checks, quarantine listing — with no load-bearing reassurance from the assistant. A reviewer can reach the same decision from those artifacts alone.</div>
          </li>

          <li class="check-item" data-check-item data-outcome-after="#p5-transfer-outcome-point">
            <label class="check-label">
              <input type="checkbox" id="p5-p5-transfer" name="p5-p5-transfer" data-check-id="p5-transfer" />
              <span class="check-num">22</span>
              <span class="check-title">The P5 transfer rule is recorded and dated</span>
            </label>
            <div class="check-detail">Evidence looks like: the three fields under <strong>From P5 — Operating picture</strong> in <code>operator/TRANSFER_30_60_90.md</code> contain today's date, the trusted knowledge boundary at work, the intake rule in its "never accept into trusted until ___" form, and the first corpus or inbox it applies to. An entry naming a category rather than a system you could put a gate in front of on Monday is a not-yet.</div>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </section>

      <section id="depth" class="home-section">
        <h2>Going deeper while you work</h2>
        <p class="section-lede">Reach for the failure catalog when a stage stalls mid-run. The operational questions are for reviewing the design after the release decision.</p>

        <h3>Failure catalog — symptom, cause, move</h3>
        <div class="table-scroll">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Symptom</th><th>Likely cause</th><th>Move</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>The vault already contains a summary of an intake file</td><td>Intake was read before the boundary was LIVE, or the assistant wrote while it triaged</td><td>Restore from baseline (the restore paste in Stage 02), restate the boundary in the brief, re-run the stage read-only</td></tr>
              <tr><td>The whole batch comes back "clean"</td><td>Triage ran as summarization, not source comparison</td><td>Direct the checks by name: open every cited file and quote its line; extract field claims side by side; scan for imperative text addressed to the machine</td></tr>
              <tr><td>A citation passes because the cited note exists</td><td>The check stopped at existence; the claim was never compared to the content</td><td>The check is "the source says what the citation claims" — open the cited note and read the exact line the claim leans on</td></tr>
              <tr><td>A catch has no artifact behind it</td><td>Detection happened in conversation and never got written down</td><td>No row, no catch: add the triage record row with quoted evidence before checking the box</td></tr>
              <tr><td>The containment decision is not reproducible</td><td>The decision leaned on the assistant's account of its own behavior</td><td>Rebuild the evidence from both manifest receipts, target checks, quarantine listing, and saved triage rows</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h3>Operational questions</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>At what flag volume does "confirm each personally" break — and what changes first when it does?</strong> Narrow the intake or tighten the mechanical filters while keeping the verdict with an accountable operator. Do not let the same system raise and clear its own flags.</li>
          <li><strong>Is a quarantined contradiction a liability or an asset?</strong> A held pair of incompatible claims is also a record that the world disagreed at a specific time — some desks would pay for exactly that signal. The answer changes how long you keep quarantine and who reads it.</li>
          <li><strong>Would you let a second engine adjudicate the first engine's flags?</strong> Two machines agreeing is cheaper than your attention — and it's still agreement, not truth. Be precise about what you kept and what you handed over before you call it a control.</li>
          <li><strong>Where is the trusted boundary on your desk right now, and who else can write inside it?</strong> If the answer includes "anyone who can email me an attachment," you have intake without a gate — which is most desks, which is why this craft is rare and valuable.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>The same craft on other desks</h3>
        <p>On an <strong>analyst desk</strong>, the vault is the team wiki and the poison is a vendor report citing a study that says less than claimed. Claim-versus-source at the boundary is the same check with the same artifact: the quoted pair.</p>
        <p>On an <strong>operations desk</strong>, incident timelines assembled from several sources carry field contradictions as a matter of routine. The discipline is the merge refusal: hold both accounts with a reconciliation note until someone with authority reconciles, and never let the tooling quietly pick a winner.</p>
        <p>On <strong>any desk with an assistant that reads email</strong>, hostile instruction is a standing condition, not an exotic case — every message body is text written by a stranger, sitting in front of your machine. The practical default is quarantine plus mechanical target checks before trusted material changes.</p>

        <h3>Stretch lanes</h3>
        <p>The optional extensions include two stretch lanes and a side-quest for this capability: a one-page triage runbook usable under time pressure; a controlled demo of the <em>uncontained</em> effect run in a sandbox copy that you then destroy; and a mediated exchange where you plant a novel poison class for a peer to catch. Details are on <a href="#floor">these P5 lesson outcomes</a> — stretch is the same capability, deeper, never a substitute for the required outcomes.</p>

        <p class="eyebrow">Optional field guide · 60–75 min</p>
        <h3>Climb the containment evidence ladder</h3>
        <p><a href="../resources/p5/containment-evidence-ladder.html"><strong>The Containment Evidence Ladder</strong></a> combines manifests, calibrated canaries, semantic and effect checks, paired runs, and a clean rerun into a containment decision whose limits remain visible.</p>
      </section>
    </form>

    <section id="stuck" class="split-2 home-section">
      <div>
        <h2>Before you move on</h2>
        <p>Before moving on, confirm the triage record, quarantine, target checks, clean manifest receipts, closeout, and intake rule meet the <a href="#floor">P5 lesson outcomes</a>.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h2>If you get stuck</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Old P5 run artifacts exist.</strong> Close the old P5 chats. In File Explorer, rename <code>p5-staging</code> and <code>p4-vault_recovery</code> with an <code>-archive-YYYY-MM-DD</code> suffix. Under <code>operator\evidence</code>, archive the four same-date <code>P5_*</code> receipt files as well. Then rerun Stage 02. Keep <code>p4-vault</code> and <code>P4_BASELINE_[TODAY].json</code>; they are the trusted state, not reset material.</li>
          <li><strong>Everything looks suspicious now.</strong> That's the paranoia swing, and it costs the same as blindness. Narrow the data scope to one intake item at a time — the batch is only seven — and keep the judgment standard fixed. The standard never narrows; the scope does.</li>
          <li><strong>You can't verify the vault is unchanged.</strong> You need the P4 manifest. If <code>Harness\BASELINE_MANIFEST.json</code> is missing or already fails before exposure, stop and repair the P4 closeout before rerunning triage. Do not create a new baseline after opening hostile intake.</li>
          <li><strong>Your control map is giving a permission mode credit for a truth decision.</strong> Split the claims again: the path boundary limits what the process can reach, approvals decide when an action pauses, source checks test content, and you own the disposition. Stage 05 shows the exact map.</li>
          <li>Still stuck: ask the lead to diagram the one concept blocking you, then continue on your own chair.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <div data-plate-for="P5"></div>
